==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/tɪl/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*tila-}}, whence also Old Frisian ''til'', Old High German {{term|zil|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Ziel|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|tilr|lang=non}}, Gothic {{term|𐍄𐌹𐌻|tr=til|lang=got}}.

====Adjective====
{{head|ang|adjective}}

# [[good]] (morally good; competent; useful, etc)
#: '''''Til''' sceal mid '''tilum'''. &mdash; The good shall be with the good.

===Etymology 2===
Possibly from {{etyl|non|ang}} {{term|til||lang=non}}<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|till}}</ref> tho the OED has it as "Germanic" and related to {{etyl|non|-}} and to {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{term|til|lang=ofs}}<ref>{{R:OED|till}}</ref> <!--http://oxforddictionaries.com/definition/till-->

====Preposition====
{{head|ang|preposition}}

# [[to]], [[until]], [[unto]]
#: ''Ðá cueð '''til''' him ðe Hǽlend'' &mdash; Then quoth unto them the Savior.

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|g=n|pl=tilas}}

# use, service, convenience
#: ''Gewritu secgaþ ðæt seó wiht sý mid moncynne miclum '''ticlum''' sweotol and gesýne, sundorcræft hafaþ. &mdash; Writings say that those beings were to mankind of great service; they clearly and obviously had special powers.
# goodness, kindness
#: ''Me on ðínum '''tile''' gelǽr ðæt ic teala cunne ðín sóðfæst weorc healdan.'' &mdash; Thy goodness teaches me that I should maintain your righteous work.

